      },
      "findings": [
        {
          "source_quote": "Ἀξιῶ σε ἐπὶ πάντων μου τῶν συνπαρόντων ἀδελφῶν, ὅπως ὁπόταν τοῦ ἐνταῦθα (ὡς ἐμοὶ ὀφείλεται) ἀπαλλάξω βίου, σὲ Ἰακώβῳ τῷ ἀδελφῷ τοῦ κυρίου διαπέμψαι ἐν ἐπιτομῇ ἀναγραψάμενον μέχρι καὶ τῶν ἐκ παίδων σου λογισμῶν καὶ ὡς ἀπ᾽ ἀρχῆς μέχρι τοῦ νῦν συνώδευσάς μοι, ἐπακούων τῶν κατὰ πόλιν ὑπ᾽ ἐμοῦ κηρυχθέντων λόγων τε καὶ πράξεων, ἔπειτα πρὸς τῷ τέλει καὶ τὴν τοῦ θανάτου μου πρόφασιν ὡς προεῖπον δηλῶσαι μὴ ὀκνήσῃς",
          "english_quote": "Before all my brothers who are present with me, I ask you that, whenever I depart from this life here—as is owed to me—you not hesitate to send James, the Lord’s brother, a concise written account extending even to your thoughts from childhood and telling how, from the beginning until now, you have traveled with me, listening to the words and deeds proclaimed by me from city to city, and then, toward the end, also to make known the cause of my death, as I said beforehand.",
          "issue": "The rendering defensibly takes the second σέ as a resumed subject of διαπέμψαι and ἀναγραψάμενον, understands the written account as the thing sent, and gives μὴ ὀκνήσῃς scope over both infinitival instructions. Nevertheless, those syntactic relationships remain unsettled, as the draft's blocking source_issues note acknowledges. Disclosure alone does not establish this construction or eliminate the alternatives. The unusual syntax does not itself demonstrate corruption.",
          "suggested_revision": "Obtain a separate grammatical assessment, supported by cited comparable constructions, of the resumed accusative after ὅπως and the temporal clause, the dependency of διαπέμψαι, and the scope of the final μὴ ὀκνήσῃς. Retain the provisional interpretation and alternatives until that evidence is assessed. Do not import wording from another edition or normalize the Greek to produce the preferred syntax. Review any revised English afresh.",
          "severity": "blocking"
        },
        {
          "source_quote": "οὐκ ἀμαθὴς ἀνὴρ καὶ ζωοποιοὺς λόγους ἀγνοῶν καὶ ἐκκλησίας κανόνα. οὐκ εἰδὼς τὴν τοῦ διδάσκοντος ἐπιστεύθη καθέδραν",
          "english_quote": "after me it was not to an unlearned man—one ignorant of life-giving words and not knowing the church’s rule—that the teacher’s chair was entrusted.",
          "issue": "The main text gives the first οὐκ broad scope over the entrusting to the described recipient while preserving the explicit negation in οὐκ εἰδώς. This is coherent, but the competing local scope of the first negation remains unresolved. The draft discloses that blocking uncertainty. The printed period after κανόνα is a separate editorial feature, not evidence establishing either scope and not by itself evidence of corruption.",
          "suggested_revision": "Provide a separate syntactic assessment of the first negation's scope across the coordinated descriptions and delayed ἐπιστεύθη, supported by comparable Greek usage. Preserve both negations and the distinction between interpretive uncertainty and printed punctuation. Do not silently distribute the first negation across the participles, delete either negation, or treat the punctuation as a transcription error. Keep the passage blocked until the assessment and any resulting English undergo fresh review.",
          "severity": "blocking"
        }
      ]
    },
